# Prose → SemanticCheckpoint extraction contract

Deterministic extraction schema for compiling an accepted future-state
narrative (Vision statement, Working-Backwards Press Release) into
`sj:GoalCheckpoint` instances over the canonical graph. The narrative stays
expressive; this contract makes it rigorous afterward. Authority of this
projection is NONE: extraction produces candidate semantics; admission happens
only through the pack shapes and gates.

## Input

One accepted prose document (Vision or WBPR). It must state, in ordinary
language: what exists, who acts, what happens, what results, and which
previous problems no longer occur. Prose that cannot answer "what observation
would permit us to stop?" is underspecified and is refused as input
(`REFUSED:UNDER_SPECIFIED_FUTURE`), not guessed into semantics.

## Extraction table (prose concept → semantic projection)

| Prose concept            | Semantic projection                  |
|--------------------------|--------------------------------------|
| noun                     | entity/type                          |
| named participant        | role/actor                           |
| verb                     | capability/transition                |
| "when ..."               | trigger                              |
| "automatically ..."      | zero-human edge                      |
| "without ..."            | exclusion (`sj:exclusion`)           |
| "every ..."              | universal invariant                  |
| "never ..."              | forbidden transition                 |
| "can ..."                | capability                           |
| "must ..."               | obligation                           |
| "because ..."            | causal/rationale relation            |
| measurable result        | postcondition (`sj:acceptance`)      |
| user quote               | experiential acceptance criterion    |
| before/after             | state transition                     |
| evidence claim           | verifier/receipt requirement         |
| launch date              | checkpoint horizon                   |
| future improvement       | successor goal (`sj:successorPolicy`)|

## Output format

For each stop-worthy proposition group, emit one `sj:GoalCheckpoint` instance
(dual-typed `sj:Checkpoint`) in the consumer `docs/sjira/<milestone>/` court
graph with exactly these fields (admission shape:
`shapes/work-order.shacl.ttl` `sj:GoalCheckpointShape`):

- `dcterms:identifier` — `G<n>` court id
- `dcterms:title`, `dcterms:description` — the proposition in prose
- `sj:subject` — exact subject the postcondition is observed on
- `sj:standing` — initial `UNKNOWN`; promotes only from court evidence
- `sj:evidenceCeiling` — strongest evidence this goal admits
- `sj:gapClass` — one of `bootstrap | first_mile | core | last_mile`
- `sj:requiresCourt` — the court that decides it
- `sj:acceptance` — the observed postcondition(s)
- `sj:falsifier` — the observation that kills the claim
- `sj:exclusion` — what does NOT keep this goal open
- `sj:successorPolicy` — where post-STOP improvements go
- `sj:llmRetirementRequirement` — the KNOWN-class condition under which no
  LLM reasoning is required on this path
- `sj:replayIdentity` — stable replay key
- `sj:nextCheckpoint` — the next gate in the ladder (last gate: omit)

## Boundary classes

Every extracted requirement is classified into exactly one gap class:

- `bootstrap` — cold-start reconstruction from durable artifacts
- `first_mile` — goal → admitted, exactly-subjected, falsifier-bearing work
- `core` — deterministic execution of KNOWN work (no LLM)
- `last_mile` — consequence observed, receipted, replayed, frontier recomputed

Debugging rule: repeated LLM reasoning in `core` locates missing
bootstrap/first-mile/last-mile machinery — it never justifies a smarter agent.

## Stopping law

The ladder stops when `gates/070_stop_court.rq` returns zero rows over the
court graph: every checkpoint ALIVE on a receipt, every falsifier unfired,
every exclusion intact. Anything newly discovered afterwards attaches to a
successor checkpoint; it never reopens a closed one.
